<div class="article-body">

<p>Brown, papery edges on the inner leaves of your lettuce are tipburn, and the calcium bottle everyone recommends will not fix it. The short answer: tipburn is a calcium <strong>delivery</strong> failure, not a calcium shortage. Your reservoir almost certainly has enough calcium sitting in it. The inner leaves just never got the water flow that carries that calcium to them.</p>

<p>I know how that sounds because I bought the cal-mag bottle too. Twice. I doubled the dose, watched the EC climb, and by day 38 I still had 34 browning leaves across 6 heads. Then a $12 clip fan mostly ended it, which made me a little angry about the money I spent on the bottle.</p>

<p>Full disclosure: I am not a plant scientist. I am a guy with four shelves in a spare room who has killed a lot of lettuce. But this is the one problem where the popular fix is almost backwards, and once you see why, you cannot unsee it.</p>

<h2 id="what">1. What Tipburn Actually Is (Inner Leaves, Not Outer)</h2>

<p>Tipburn shows up as a dead, papery brown edge on the leaves in the <strong>center</strong> of the head. The young leaves that were still folded inside each other. They curl a little, the margin dries out, and no matter how much you water, those specific edges stay dead. Dead tissue does not come back.</p>

<p>Here is the key insight: which leaves brown tells you which problem you have. Inner and young leaves going brown first is tipburn, a calcium delivery problem. Outer and bottom leaves going yellow or brown first is something else entirely, usually a mobile nutrient or a root issue. If your older leaves are the ones changing color, this is not your article. Start with <a href="/posts/hydroponic-lettuce-not-growing">why your hydroponic lettuce is not growing</a> and work from there.</p>

<p><strong>It is also not a disease.</strong> No fungus, no bacteria, nothing contagious. You cannot catch it from your own plant and it will not spread to the tray next to it as an infection. That matters, because a lot of new growers throw out a whole tray for a cosmetic disorder they could have trimmed off with scissors.</p>

<h2 id="calmag">2. Why the Cal-Mag Bottle Does Nothing</h2>

<p>Calcium is an immobile nutrient in the plant. It cannot be pulled from an old leaf and shipped to a new one the way nitrogen can. It only travels one way: dissolved in water, carried upward by transpiration, the water vapor leaving the leaves. No water moving through a leaf means no calcium arriving in that leaf, even if your reservoir is perfectly balanced.</p>

<p>Go read the Reddit threads on this and you will see the same sentence over and over from people who actually grow: you are usually not deficient, the plant just cannot transpire correctly. That is the whole thing. The calcium is there. The delivery truck is not running.</p>

<p>I thought I had it figured out. So I bought a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=cal+mag+supplement+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">cal-mag supplement</a> and ran it at 1.5 mL per liter on top of my normal nutrients.</p>

<p>Here is the part that actually surprised me. The bottle made it worse. My solution went from an EC of 1.4 to 2.1, and the browning count barely moved: 34 leaves on day 38, still 34 leaves on day 44. Higher EC means a saltier solution, which makes it <em>harder</em> for roots to pull water in. So the bottle raised the exact number that was blocking the calcium delivery in the first place. I had paid $22 to make my own problem slightly worse.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Quick Math</div>
  <p>The bottle: $22, and it pushed EC from 1.4 to 2.1. One dose of cal-mag at 1.5 mL/L added roughly 0.3 to 0.7 EC depending on the brand. For lettuce, 1.2 to 1.6 EC is the working range. So a "calcium fix" can push you out of range and slow the water uptake you were trying to improve.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="cause">3. The Real Cause: Your Inner Leaves Live in Still Air</h2>

<p>A leaf transpires when the air right around it is drier than the leaf. That dry layer of air touching the leaf surface is the boundary layer, and if it never moves, it fills up with water vapor until it matches the leaf. At that point transpiration stops cold. The leaf is not dry. It is sitting in its own humid little bubble.</p>

<p>Now think about the center of a lettuce head. Those young leaves are physically wrapped inside older leaves. They have the thickest boundary layer in the whole plant by default. In a closed room with no air movement and 80% humidity, that center can sit at saturation all day. Zero transpiration, zero calcium delivery, dead edges. The outer leaves are fine because they touch moving air.</p>

<p>Pay attention to this number. Airflow of 0.3 to 1.0 m/s across the canopy is the range that shows up again and again in the research, and one 2025 study in HortScience found that downward airflow of 0.4 to 1.3 m/s cut tipburn incidence and severity in vertical farms without costing any biomass. You get the same yield with fewer ruined leaves. High humidity does the opposite, and high EC makes the water harder to pull, which is why the bottle backfired.</p>

<p>And here is why that matters: a 2025 Frontiers in Plant Science study found a calcium-mobilizing biostimulant controlled tipburn about as well as vertical airflow fans. Even the product that works proves the point. It is not making calcium appear. It is helping calcium move.</p>

<h2 id="fix">4. The $12 Fix That Stopped Mine in 6 Days</h2>

<p>I clipped a small <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=oscillating+clip+fan+for+grow+tent&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">oscillating clip fan</a> above the canopy, aimed slightly downward so it brushes the tops of the heads, and left it running 24 hours a day. Six days later the new inner leaves came in clean. The heads that were already scarred stayed scarred, because dead tissue does not heal, but nothing new browned.</p>

<p>Three settings did most of the work:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Air, but not at the reservoir.</strong> Above the canopy, oscillating. A fan blasting straight into the tank just spikes evaporation, drops your water level, and concentrates your nutrients, which raises EC and starts the whole problem over.</li>
  <li><strong>Humidity between 60% and 70%.</strong> I used a $13 <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=thermo+hygrometer+indoor+humidity+monitor&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">thermo-hygrometer</a> and mine was sitting at 82% on the shelf. Above roughly 75% the inner leaves barely transpire. A small <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=small+humidifier+for+plants&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">room humidifier</a> actually helps here in reverse: it stops the swings. I run it low, more for stability than for fog.</li>
  <li><strong>EC between 1.2 and 1.6.</strong> I check mine with a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=ec+tds+meter+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">cheap EC meter</a> twice a week. Lettuce does not want a hot solution. If you are running the same mix as your tomatoes, that is your whole problem.</li>
</ul>

<p>Light matters too, just less directly. I had my lettuce on a 20 hour photoperiod and a DLI near 21, which is a lot of growth for leaves that were not getting calcium to their newest edges. Cutting to 14 hours and about 15 DLI is what finally gave the fan a chance to catch up. If you have not dialed that in yet, read <a href="/posts/how-much-light-does-lettuce-need">how much light lettuce actually needs indoors</a> before you buy another bottle of anything.</p>

<p>Where I landed over the next 8 weeks: 34 browning leaves across 6 heads went to 3 across the next 6, and 4 of those 6 heads were fully marketable with untrimmed edges. Same seed, same nutrients, same everything except moving air and a saner EC.</p>

<h2 id="eat">5. Can You Still Eat It? Yes. Here Is How to Trim It</h2>

<p>Tipburn is cosmetic. There is no pathogen and no toxin. Trim the dead edge with scissors, about 2 to 3 mm past the brown line so you get clean green tissue, and eat the rest. Those leaves sometimes carry a faint bitterness right at the damaged margin, which is another reason to cut a little deeper than the visible browning.</p>

<p>One honest caveat: if the brown has gone wet, slimy or smelly, that is no longer tipburn, that is rot, and that head goes in the bin. Dry and papery means trim. Soft and foul means toss. Same color, opposite answer.</p>

<p>Here is the honest truth about my setup: the clip fan is not quiet. It is a $12 fan in a guest room, and you can hear it through a closed door at night. It also clips to whatever you clip it to, so on a thin shelf it rattles unless you add a bit of foam tape. If silence matters more to you than $30, a tower fan placed a few feet away works just as well and is much quieter, it just takes more floor space and it moves less air right at the canopy.</p>

<p>If you are just starting, do not buy cal-mag for tipburn. Buy moving air and a way to measure EC. Those two things fixed a problem I spent a month and $22 trying to fix from the other direction. The fan I use is usually $12 to $16, and the meter runs $10 to $20. Check price on the fan; if it is under $16, grab it, because the quiet ones go up fast in summer.</p>

<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> tipburn means the calcium is not arriving, not that the calcium is missing. Move the air, drop the EC, hold the humidity, and trim the old scars off with scissors. Then start the next batch with <a href="/posts/best-lettuce-varieties-for-indoors">lettuce varieties that actually regrow indoors</a>, because a variety that holds its head tight is a variety that hides its inner leaves from moving air. Need the light side sorted too? <a href="/grow-lights">Compare grow lights by real specs</a> before you spend anything else.</p>

